is would also defend d4 but is a passive move that blocks the natural development of the Queen's Knight to c3 (though in this specific pawn structure, c3 is acceptable, developing a piece is preferred).\n- `N: b1-c3`: This develops a piece but does NOT defend d4, leading to the loss of the pawn.\n- `B: c1-g5`: While it attacks the Queen, it is unsupported and would simply be captured by the Queen (`Qxg5`).\n\nReason: `N: g1-f3` is the most principled move. It addresses the immediate threat to the d4 pawn by reinforcing it, while simultaneously developing a key piece and preparing for castling, which allows White to consolidate the material advantage safely.\n\nAnswer: N: g1-f3"}
