e dangerous) and activates the h1 Rook. Other options like developing the Knight (N: b1-c3) or pushing the pawn (P: d4-d5) are reasonable, but securing the King is strategically superior and restricts Black's counterplay opportunities. There are no immediate tactical wins (e.g., attacks on f7 are defended), so improving the King's position is the best long-term strategy.\n\nReason: Castling is legal (f1 is not attacked by the Queen on f6 due to the pawn on f2 shielding it, and g1/e1 are safe) and immediately improves White's safety and piece coordination. It punishes Black's lack of King safety by consolidating White's own position before launching offensive operations in the center.\n\nAnswer: K: e1-g1"}
